abstract: This metadata entry contains information about the Integrated Coastal Observatory
  (ICO), which was a coordinated network of partners along the coast of British Columbia
  who are using eDNA to monitor marine biodiversity. This diverse network includes
  First Nations, government scientists, citizen scientists, and NGOs. At the core
  of this initiative is the desire to generate high-quality open data that can be
  used by our partners to quantify regional biodiversity. Environmental DNA samples
  from seawater are collected monthly to track the diversity of fish present at each
  partner location site. The resulting data provide an important tool for monitoring
  changes in diversity over broad spatial and temporal scales. Our results demonstrate
  the advantages of democratizing the collection and dissemination of eDNA-based biodiversity
  data. Long-term eDNA-based monitoring in coastal BC will help us to measure ecosystem
  health and track the impacts of climate change and other disturbances on marine
  biodiversity.
